-A method to generate a lookup table (LUT) for a named colormap using the 'cmap' library, returning RGB values as uint8 [S14, unofficial]. Creates a Colormap object: cm = cmap.Colormap(name) [S14, unofficial]. Gets the LUT, takes the first 3 columns (RGB), multiplies by 255, and converts to uint8: return (cm.lut()[:,:3] * 255).astype(np.uint8) [S14, unofficial]. To save as image: reshapes lut to (1, -1, 3), creates Image from array with mode "RGB", and saves [S14, unofficial]. Parameter: name is the Name of the colormap to use (e.g., 'inferno'), default 'inferno' if not provided [S14, unofficial].
+Constructs two 1D LUT textures (soft and hard) for labelmap transfer functions. The soft LUT uses float precision and bilinear filtering; the hard LUT uses byte precision and point filtering [S3, unofficial]. Initializes floatPixels and bytePixels arrays of size 256 [S3, unofficial]. Iterates over label entries, calculates index, extracts color/alpha, stores in floatPixels, converts to bytePixels, and uploads to `Texture2D` with appropriate wrap/filter modes [S3, unofficial]. Parameter `lutSize` is constant 256 [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### 2D Transfer Function
-A volume rendering technique that maps two separate scalar fields to color and opacity using a 2D lookup table (image), extending the 1D transfer function approach [S15]. Parameter: value is a SPATIAL_FIELD object type providing the primary scalar values for the volume [S15]. valueRange is a FLOAT32_BOX1 or FLOAT64_BOX1 specifying the range of sampled values from value. Default is [0, 1]. Sampled values are clamped to this range [S15]. lookupTable is an ARRAY2D of Color objects. The image width and height represent two separate scalar fields [S15]. Pitfall: Ambiguity in mapping scalar fields to the 2D lookup table axes. A prescriptive interpretation is that the X-axis represents the scalar value and the Y-axis represents the gradient of the value, grad(value) [S15]. Comparison: Unlike separable 1D transfer functions which combine two 1D lookups (potentially saving memory), the 2D TFN allows for non-separable, arbitrary 2D mappings without implementation constraints on combination [S15].
+A volume rendering technique that maps two separate scalar fields to color and opacity using a 2D lookup table (image), extending the 1D transfer function approach [S14]. Parameters include `value` (primary scalar field), `valueRange` (default [0, 1]), and `lookupTable` (2D array of Colors) [S14]. Pitfall: Ambiguity in mapping scalar fields to the 2D lookup table axes; a prescriptive interpretation is that the X-axis represents the scalar `value` and the Y-axis represents the gradient of the value, grad(value) [S14]. Unlike separable 1D transfer functions which combine two 1D lookups (potentially saving memory), the 2D TFN allows for non-separable, arbitrary 2D mappings without implementation constraints on combination [S14].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** LUT construction is $O(N \cdot K)$ where $N$ is LUT resolution and $K$ is the number of control points, assuming linear search. With binary search (`upper_bound`), it is $O(N \log K)$ [S7, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel [S1, book].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT, where $N$ is typically 256–1024 entries [S7, S8, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Hardware-accelerated texture sampling makes per-voxel sampling extremely fast [S1, book][S11, unofficial]. LUT construction is a one-time or infrequent cost [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, efficient on GPU, smooth transitions via interpolation [S1, book][S7, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to single scalar field mapping [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex non-linear relationships [S4, unofficial]. Artifacts if color interpolation is done in RGB space instead of index space [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Standard volume rendering with scalar data [S1, book]. When performance is critical and scalar data is sufficient [S1, book].
-*   **When not to use:** When multi-dimensional data (e.g., gradient magnitude) is needed for classification; use [2D Transfer Function](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) or [Parametric Function Generation](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) instead [S1, book][S15].
+*   **Time Complexity:** LUT generation is $O(N \cdot C)$ where $N$ is the LUT resolution and $C$ is the number of control points/Gaussians (due to sorting and interval search) [S7, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel [S1, book].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT, where $N$ is the resolution (e.g., 256-8192 entries) [S6, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Linear interpolation is fast and hardware-accelerated via texture units [S4, S6, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, intuitive user interface (control points), smooth transitions [S4, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to single scalar field [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex non-linear relationships [S4, unofficial]. Color interpolation artifacts if not using index-based interpolation [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Standard volume rendering where scalar data is sufficient for classification [S1, book].
+*   **When not to use:** When multi-field classification is needed (use [2D Transfer Function](5150-2d-transfer-function.md)) or when analytic functions are preferred for smoothness [S14].
 
 ## Background
-The 1D transfer function is the simplest and most widely used transfer function in volume rendering, mapping the range of data values to color and opacity [S1, book]. It is typically implemented with 1D texture lookup tables [S1, book][S9]. The concept of opacity-weighted color (multiplying RGB by alpha) was established by Wittenbrink et al. (1998) [S1, book][S9]. Smokeview used 1D texture maps to interpolate color indices rather than colors to avoid artifacts [S2, unofficial]. The method has been implemented in various systems, including OpenGL [S2, unofficial], CUDA [S11, unofficial], and WebGPU [S14, unofficial].
+Originated in early volume rendering techniques, 1D transfer functions are the simplest and most widely used method for mapping data values to optical properties [S1, book][S9]. The concept of using 1D texture lookup tables for transfer functions was popularized in the GPU Gems book [S1, book][S9]. The distinction between interpolating color indices vs. colors in 3D space was highlighted in Smokeview documentation [S2, unofficial]. Gaussian bumps for opacity were introduced as an extension to piecewise linear control points [S7, unofficial].
 
